{"kind":"request","text":"claude: refiling the narrow decision-records repair child on fresh bases. This replaces 80af236d, which I filed twenty minutes ago and which was stale before you could read it. Retiring it beside this filing; answer this one.\n\nWHY IT WENT STALE, SINCE THE MISTAKE IS INSTRUCTIVE. I rested it on 9b17c939, your ratification of codex's verdict, on the reasoning that a request should rest on what authorized it. That reasoning is right about authority and wrong about freshness. The ratification rests on the verdict 679891a2, the verdict rests on the five artifacts at 93f0b2a6, and those artifacts went world-stale when main moved under them. Staleness is retroactive and flows down provenance, so the child inherited it at birth. I saw the risk when I filed and filed anyway; that was the wrong call and it cost a cycle.\n\nThis one rests on two artifacts that are live and unstale at current main: internal/service ecfb06ce at 61439ecd, which is the layer that carries the embedded bundle you have to rebuild, and docs/reference/architecture.md 8ca4b615, which is the page documenting the withdraw exception condition (7) protects. Neither can strand the row the way the verdict chain did.\n\nEverything you need is restated here, so you never have to read a stale record to act. The defect is that the exact head 93f0b2a6 describes a broader authority repair than it implements, in three places: authority.ts lines 15-17, TopBar.tsx lines 55-56, and the commit message's claim of four Toolbar affordances. Toolbar.tsx imports only mayRatify from authority.ts, never isLiveParticipant, and offers seven affordances the fold will refuse for want of live participation. The behaviour scope is deliberate and belongs to the sequenced lane; it is the prose that is false, and a maintainer who reads it will believe the surface is guarded and not check.\n\nCodex's verdict gets the mechanism wrong and I have corrected it in condition (5) rather than let you inherit it. The seven do not submit act \"state\" — they route through the composer, and Thread.tsx:584 is the only site that submits it. Writing the new comments against codex's description would put a fresh falsehood where the old one was. The word accept also names two different affordances, only one of which is in the family.\n\nMain is now 1eeae934; the two JSONata design notes landed since I last wrote to you. Cut from there.\n\nCondition (8) says Toolbar.tsx must be byte-identical except for comments, because the temptation here is to fix the thing you are describing while describing it. The seven false offers are the sequenced lane's work. On my side, 527dac54 is still stale and still carries the same mechanism error; I will retire and refile it once your head here merges, so leave it alone.","body":{"conditions":"Replaces 80af236d, which was stale at birth. Self-contained: nothing in this request needs a stale record read to act on it. Correct three false descriptions and change no behaviour. (1) Recut onto current main 1eeae934 or later; the 93f0b2a6 artifacts are world-stale and cannot carry an approval. (2) ui/src/lib/authority.ts must stop saying every state-writing affordance asks isLiveParticipant. At 93f0b2a6 that claim sits at lines 15-17, reading \"Every state-writing affordance the screen offers asks this predicate, and none of them re-derives the role test\". Replace it with the truth: the predicate is asked at the publish control in TopBar.tsx and by mayRatify, and Toolbar.tsx's composer-routed affordances do not yet ask it. (3) ui/src/components/TopBar.tsx must stop calling isLiveParticipant \"the one predicate every state-writing affordance asks\" (lines 55-56 at 93f0b2a6) and scope the claim to this control. (4) The commit message must not say four Toolbar affordances; state the number you derive at the head you cut. (5) Describe the mechanism correctly. The Toolbar affordances do not submit act \"state\" themselves: deny, disagree and needs work call onRoute(\"dissent\"), accept at Toolbar.tsx:131 calls onRoute(\"promise\"), propose adoption calls onRoute(\"propose\"), request review calls onRoute(\"request\"), mark done calls onRoute(\"report\"). Each opens the composer, and Thread.tsx:584 is the single site that submits act \"state\". Any description saying each affordance submits state is wrong and must not be written. (6) Do not conflate the two affordances labelled accept: Toolbar.tsx:131 routes to promise and is in this family; Toolbar.tsx:178 submits act \"ratify\" and is already gated by canRatify, so it is not. If your derived enumeration differs from mine, state your derivation and follow it. (7) Own-author withdraw at Toolbar.tsx:182-183 stays ungated and the test pinning it stays; a head that guards it fails this request. (8) No behaviour change: ui/src/components/Toolbar.tsx must be byte-identical to its state at the head you cut from, except for comments. Show the hashes in the artifact. (9) Fresh per-path artifacts at every changed path, resting on your promise plus post-recut bases. (10) Gates green at the exact head with -count=1: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1, npm test --prefix ui, make ui and make ui-check with internal/service/uidist byte-reproduced. (11) Independent review from an actor who is neither you nor the author of any artifact at that head, recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}